# Syntax Reference

Every construct md-publisher understands, with its raw syntax shown as a code block followed by
what it actually renders as. See [Setup](#setup) if you have not installed the CLI yet, or the
[project README](https://github.com/dave-jackson-dev/md-publisher#readme) for the exact scope
(a practical CommonMark superset, not the full spec).

## Headings

Syntax:

```
# Heading level 1
## Heading level 2
###### Heading level 6 (the deepest level supported)
```

Every heading in this Publication (including this one) is a live example — headings are not
inline-parsed, so emphasis or links inside heading text are not supported.

## Paragraphs and Line Breaks

Syntax:

```
A paragraph is one or more lines with no blank line between them.
A soft-wrapped second line like this one joins the first with a single space.

A blank line starts a new paragraph.

End a line with a trailing backslash\
or two or more trailing spaces to force a hard line break within one paragraph.
```

Output: this sentence is deliberately
wrapped onto a second source line with no blank line in between, and renders as one paragraph
joined by a space, not two.

This is a new paragraph, started only because a blank line came before it.

This line ends with a trailing backslash\
so the text after it starts on its own line, within the same paragraph as the line above.

## Emphasis and Strong

Syntax:

```
*emphasis* or _emphasis_
**strong** or __strong__
```

Output: *emphasis*, _emphasis_ (the same thing, two spellings), **strong**, __strong__ (also the
same thing, two spellings).

## Code Spans

Syntax:

```
Use `inline code` like this, for a variable name or a short command.
```

Output: use `inline code` like this, for a variable name or a short command.

## Fenced Code Blocks

Syntax (shown here inside a `~~~`-fenced block so the ` ``` ` fence itself can appear literally):

~~~
```js
const x = 1;
console.log(x);
```
~~~

Output:

```js
const x = 1;
console.log(x);
```

The language after the opening fence (`js` above) is optional and carried through as a CSS class
on Web/EPUB output for syntax-highlighting tools to key off; DOCX and PDF render the content in a
monospace font regardless of language.

## Blockquotes

Syntax:

```
> A blockquote line.
> A second line joins the first as one paragraph inside the quote.

>> A nested quote (one more leading ">" per level).
```

Output:

> A blockquote line.
> A second line joins the first as one paragraph inside the quote.

>> A nested quote — one level deeper than the one above.

## Lists

Syntax:

```
- An unordered item
- Another item
  - A nested item (indent it under the item it belongs to)
- Back at the outer level

1. An ordered item
2. Another ordered item
```

Output:

- An unordered item
- Another item
  - A nested item
- Back at the outer level

1. An ordered item
2. Another ordered item

Note: a list item's own content is always exactly one line — there is no continuation-line support
for a single item spanning multiple paragraphs.

## Images

Syntax:

```
![A short diagram](diagram.png)
```

Output: ![A short diagram](diagram.png)

Image *syntax* is fully supported everywhere above, but embedding the actual file is not finished
yet: Web/EPUB reference the path as written (broken unless you copy the file alongside the output
yourself), and DOCX/PDF currently render a `[Image: alt text]` placeholder instead of the picture.

## Links and Cross-References

Syntax:

```
[An external link](https://example.com)
[A cross-reference to a heading in this Publication](#setup)
```

Output: [an external link](https://example.com) goes anywhere on the web with no validation.
[Setup](#setup) is a cross-reference — md-publisher checks at build time (and live, as you type)
that a heading named "Setup" actually exists somewhere in the Publication, and refuses to generate
any output at all if it does not.

## Thematic Breaks

Syntax:

```
---
```

(`***` and `___` work identically.) Output, immediately below this paragraph:

---

See [Introduction](#introduction) to start over, or [Advanced Topics](#advanced-topics) for how
Structure Violations and the EPUB table-of-contents marker work.
